{"fields":{"advisory":{"aliases":[],"categories":["memory-exposure"],"collection":"crates","cvss":null,"date":"2026-10-16","description":"When decompression fails inside a legacy (zstd v0.4 to v0.7) frame, the\r\nbundled zstd library reports the output buffer as fully written although\r\nnothing was written. `DCtx::decompress_stream` committed that position even\r\non error, and for a `Vec<u8>` destination this extended the vector's length\r\nover uninitialized memory.\r\n\r\nLegacy support is enabled by default. A 5- or 6-byte corrupt legacy frame is\r\nenough: through `zstd::stream::write::Decoder`, up to 32 KiB of stale heap\r\nmemory is then passed to the wrapped writer on the next `write()` or\r\n`flush()`, or when an `auto_flush()`/`on_flush()` decoder is dropped. Whether\r\nthis reaches an attacker depends on where the application sends the\r\ndecompressed output. `zstd::stream::read::Decoder`, `zstd::decode_all` and\r\nthe bulk API are not affected.\r\n\r\nBefore zstd-safe 4.0.0, output buffers could only be initialized slices.\r\nThe zstd crate's own writer had the same flaw from 0.4.22 to 0.7.x, which is\r\ncovered by a separate advisory.\r\n\r\nThe flaw was corrected by only committing the buffer positions of a\r\nsuccessful call.","expect-deleted":false,"id":"RUSTSEC-2026-0403","informational":null,"keywords":["uninitialized-memory","decompression","legacy"],"license":"CC0-1.0","package":"zstd-safe","references":["https://github.com/gyscos/zstd-rs/pull/384"],"related":[],"source":null,"title":"Uninitialized memory exposed when streaming decompression of a legacy frame fails","url":"https://github.com/gyscos/zstd-rs/commit/006e1d1ad1eeaf6b5d4c28329e70217adabcec43","withdrawn":null},"code":"vulnerability","graphs":[{"Krate":{"name":"zstd-safe","version":"7.2.4"},"parents":[{"Krate":{"name":"zstd","version":"0.13.3"},"parents":[{"Krate":{"name":"chitchat","version":"0.11.1"},"parents":[{"Krate":{"name":"hydracache-cluster-chitchat","version":"0.73.0"},"pare